<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Sino-Japanese War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Sino-Japanese War was fought in 1894–1895 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, mainly over control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s modernised forces won decisively, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> gave Japan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and an indemnity, revealing Qing weakness and marking Japan's emergence as an imperial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war.jpg" alt="First Sino-Japanese War" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%97%85%E9%A1%BA%E6%B5%B7%E5%86%9B%E5%85%AC%E6%89%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was a Qing tributary state in the late 19th century, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, transformed by the reforms that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868, regarded it as a target of expansion. Japan opened Korea to trade by the treaty of 1876, and from 1879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Korea policy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident">Imo Incident</a> of 1882 the Qing stationed troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and sent advisers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> trained a new Korean military formation.</p><p>A reformist faction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the Enlightenment Party, looked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and in December 1884 it staged the Kapsin Coup with the help of the Japanese minister. In late April 1894 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Rebellion</a>, the largest peasant rebellion in Korean history, broke out against oppressive taxation, and the Korean government asked for Qing help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> sent between 2,465 and 2,800 troops in June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which held that it had not been notified as the 1885 Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> required, landed a larger force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a>. Japanese troops seized the Korean king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> on 23 July 1894 and set up a pro-Japanese government. On 25 July the Japanese Navy won the Battle of Pungdo and sank the transport Kowshing, which was carrying 1,200 Qing reinforcements. War was declared on 1 August.</p><p>After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> on 15 September the Qing troops withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a> was decisively defeated at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> two days later. The Japanese Army invaded Manchuria and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> on 21 November 1894, after which Japanese forces killed civilians in the Port Arthur Massacre, with unconfirmed estimates in the thousands. Weihaiwei fell on 12 February 1895 after a siege lasting 23 days. The Qing court began negotiations in early March, and Japanese forces occupied the Pescadores Islands later that month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> was signed on 17 April 1895. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s total independence and ceded the Liaodong Peninsula, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the Penghu Islands. It also paid a large indemnity. On 23 April Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> applied diplomatic pressure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> gave up the Liaodong Peninsula in exchange for a larger indemnity. Taiwan's inhabitants resisted the cession with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign lasting nearly two years.</p><p>The war exposed the limits of the Qing effort to modernise its military and shifted regional dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Historians link the defeat to the political upheavals led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> that ended in the 1911 Revolution. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the Japanese-sponsored Kabo Reforms of 1894 to 1896 abolished legal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the privileges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a> class. Tension between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Japan over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> grew in the years that followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Sino-Japanese War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b> of 1894 to 1895 and the Russo-Japanese War, which culminated in Admiral Togo's annihilation of the Russian fleet at Tsushima in May 1905, showed a consistent Japanese operational method: rapid offensive tempo, tactical aggression, and the exploitation of enemy hesitation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1894 the Donghak Peasant Revolution prompted both powers to intervene, leading to the <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat by Japan in the <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b> of 1894–1895 forced the cession of Taiwan and the recognition of Korean independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Qing and Japanese forces landed at Incheon in May, and the dispute over the Convention of Tientsin led to the <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b> (1894–1895), in which Qing China was defeated by Meiji Japan, a state that had undergone more comprehensive reforms in the same period, exposed these limits.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>First Sino-Japanese War</b> and the Russo-Japanese War the military came to see itself as a growing world power.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
